"""Class 8 SST Ch 7 Factors of Production - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4("Wages are the reward for labour.", "Labour means the physical and mental effort used in production.", "a",
     "Wages pay for the effort described in R."),
 AR4("Profit is the reward of entrepreneurship.", "Interest is the reward for capital.", "b",
     "Both are true but R does not explain A."),
 AR4("A tractor is an example of capital.", "Capital includes only money and never machines or buildings.", "c",
     "A is true. R is false: capital includes durable assets such as machines and buildings."),
 AR4("Human capital means the money invested in buying machines.", "Education, healthcare and training build human capital.", "d",
     "A is false: human capital is the skills, knowledge and expertise of people. R is true."),
 AR4("Handicrafts are labour-intensive.", "They use a larger number of workers than machines and capital.", "a",
     "R is the meaning of labour-intensive."),
 AR4("Companies in India above certain limits must spend 2 per cent of their average profits on Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR).", "Kaizen is the Japanese culture of continuous improvement.", "b",
     "Both are true but unrelated."),
]

CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> J. R. D. Tata started Tata Airlines in 1932. He took the risk of starting a new business, brought together land, labour and capital, and faced the chance of loss. He later received the Bharat Ratna in 1992. (i) Which factor of production does J. R. D. Tata represent here? (ii) What is the reward of this factor? (iii) In which year did he start Tata Airlines? (iv) Why can an entrepreneur's reward sometimes be a loss?",
   "(i) Entrepreneurship (1). (ii) Profit (1). (iii) 1932 (1). (iv) The entrepreneur bears the risk and is paid only what remains after all other factors are paid; if revenue is less than costs, there is a loss (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> A garment factory near Pune trains its workers in new stitching skills and follows the Japanese idea of kaizen, making small improvements every day. Output per worker has increased, and wages have risen. (i) What does kaizen mean? (ii) What term is used for the skills and knowledge of workers? (iii) What is the reward for labour called? (iv) Explain how training workers increases production.",
   "(i) Continuous improvement (1). (ii) Human capital (1). (iii) Wages (1). (iv) Trained workers work faster with fewer errors and can use better technology, so more is produced from the same inputs (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> About 65 per cent of India's population is below the age of 35. Platforms such as SWAYAM offer online courses, and the National Career Service helps job seekers. During COVID-19 several supply chains broke down and production stopped. (i) What share of India's population is below 35? (ii) What does SWAYAM provide? (iii) What is a supply chain? (iv) Why does a large young population become a demographic dividend only with skills and jobs?",
   "(i) About 65 per cent (1). (ii) Online courses (1). (iii) A network of people, organisations and activities that delivers a product from producers to consumers (1). (iv) Unskilled or unemployed youth cannot add to production; with skills and jobs they raise output and incomes (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

